  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/a/a4/Strange_Tales_Vol_1_116.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The Puppet Master is back (having survived what appeared to be certain death at the end of [LINK:1963:FF_14|FF #14|2026-08-06] where his sub is grabbed by a giant octopus), and he thinks he's learned his lesson on how to defeat the FF -- don't' battle them all at once -- \"Divide and conquer!\" he says while fashioning a figure of the Human Torch.\n\nHe commands the Torch to go to Alicia's, and once there he tries to sweep her off her feet!&nbsp; Ben is none too happy to see this when he arrives!&nbsp; And so the fight between the Thing and Torch begins, with the Puppet Master's work apparently done.\n\nAs the battle begins in earnest, furniture flies (and smashes), doors are torn off hinges, etc. before they finally head upstairs to the roof, Johnny simply burning a hole through the apartment ceiling.\n\nA few pages of fighting ensues before the Puppet Master sees that their friendship is holding them back from genuinely hurting each other.&nbsp; In order to do the job properly, he'll have to take a <i>personal</i> hand in the battle, which will require his physical presence.&nbsp; He mentally orders Johnny to head toward Idlewild airport and prepares to head there himself.\n\nMeanwhile, back in Alicia's apartment, the blind sculptress puts two and two together and concludes that it was the Puppet Master that made Johnny act so oddly toward her.&nbsp; He must still be <i>alive</i>!&nbsp; She jumps into action saying \"His last known hideout was near Idlewild Airport!\" (<i>gee, what a remarkable 6<sup>th</sup> sense she has</i>).\n\nAt the airport, it seems that all 4 of the characters arrive at once.&nbsp; The Puppet Master commands him directly to fly toward a jet, and while Johnny has no choice but to obey, he's able to \"fly <i>through</i> the jet's innards and hurl himself harmlessly out again through the aircraft's exhaust!\"&nbsp; (Say <i>what?</i>)\n\nAlicia instructs Johnny to lower a flaming sheet of flame down toward the Puppet Master, which melts his puppet and burns his hands.&nbsp; The Thing now realizes what happened, and all is well again, but the Puppet Master simply runs away, and Johnny doesn't bother to chase after him!",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME -- write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME -- write something",
  "review_text": "In a word:&nbsp; <i>dreadful</i>.&nbsp; Stan Lee must have decided to let a Thing/Torch battle take up a whole feature story, instead of the normal couple of pages we see in just about <i>every</i> issue of the FF thus far.\n\n[ASSET: left | PuppetMasterIsBack | width=26%] The Puppet Master is <i>back</i> (and artist Dick Ayers at least does a good job depicting him with those villainous teeth.\n[BR]\n[BR] The last time readers saw him, he was in a submarine at the end of [LINK:1963:FF_14|FF #14|2026-08-06] (published eight months earlier), about to be destroyed by a giant octopus.&nbsp; So this story had to spend a page explaining how he survived.&nbsp; (And I note this is the <i>second</i> time the Puppet Master has cheated what seemed like certain death.)\n\nJust like in [LINK:1963:FF_14|FF #14|2026-08-06], the Puppet Master doesn't actively engage with either the Human Torch or Thing.&nbsp; Rather, he serves only as the catalyst to stage the battle, in this case between Johnny and Ben.&nbsp; And he even drags his stepdaughter into the mix by mesmerizing Johnny into making a romantic gesture toward her, banking on the fact that when Thing showed up moments later and saw it, he would fly off the handle in rage.\n\n[ASSET: right | MissionAccomplished | width=17%] By the end of page three, you've pretty much seen the full extent of the Puppet Master's powers -- the ability to give Stan Lee a narrative shortcut to stage whatever battle happens to occur to him in the wee hours of the morning.\n[BR]\n[BR] (That, and the ability to blind people with those dazzling <i>teeth</i>!)\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=As a character, the Puppet Master is little more than a walking, talking plot device.]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And as a father figure, he seems to have no problem using his stepdaughter as fodder!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nBut... say what you want about the threat level of the Puppet Master, but you have to be honest:&nbsp; his ploy worked.\n\n[ASSET: left | FlyingTelevision | width=18%] The battle between the Thing and Torch takes up most of the remaining pages, but on page 5, we see that the Thing has no problem smashing things in his girlfriend's apartment, tossing aside bookcases, televisions and the like.\n[BR]\n[BR] Which raises the question:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Why does a blind girl even <i>own</i> a television when a radio would do just as well, not to mention take up a lot less space?]\n\nCommon sense questions aside, the end result is the same:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=Alicia's apartment is <i>ruined</i>, now with a big hole in the roof to <i>boot</i>!]\n\n\n\n[ASSET: center | FlawInThePlan | width=67%]\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n\nDick Ayer's artwork wasn't bad, but frankly wasn't up to Kirby's standards (not like Kirby himself would have been able to do much with this plot).&nbsp; Ayers should stick to inking.\n\n[ASSET: left | LastKnownHideout | width=12%] Alicia's sudden guess that her stepfather would be at Idlewild Airport comes <i>totally</i> out of left field, and Johnny flying <i>through</i> an aircraft?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Stan, what have you been <i>smoking</i>?]\n\nIt's almost embarrassing that I have to point out that Alicia knew <i>nothing</i> of her stepfather's criminal tendencies when we first met her back in [LINK:1962:FF_8|FF #8|2026-04-30] (something I pointed out even then was unlikely), and after his apparent death at the end of that issue, she was as surprised as everyone else to learn of return in [LINK:1963:FF_14|FF #14|2026-08-06], where he apparently dies <i>again</i>.&nbsp; And you can see in the panel <i>here</i> that she's only <i>now</i> realizing that he's still alive!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=Suddenly she knows about his \"last known hideout?\"&nbsp; (<i>Give me a <b>break</b>!</i>)]\n\n\nAnd they stand around all buddy-buddy at the end while the Puppet Master runs away on foot, with Johnny not even bothering to pursue?&nbsp; I'm sorry, but that's too much!&nbsp; This story just flat-out <b>stunk</b>, no question about it.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nI'm not a big Doctor Strange fan, but thank God I get to turn the page to the next feature and put this absolute mess of a story behind me!&nbsp; <i>Bleah!</i>",
  "side_note": [
    "The splash page acknowledges that the story was \"Based upon an idea by Tommy and Jimmy Goodkind, Hewlett Harbor, New York.\""
  ],
  "highlight": "FIXME -- write something"
}